{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And they bore children</b> — If the language is taken at its plain sense, that he returned to his wife and to his maidservants, for HaShem had closed up every womb, then it is astonishing. For it appears that even on the first night that Sarah was taken to Abimelech's house, before he had yet come near her, Elohim came to him in a dream, and in the morning he rose early and called for his servants and also for Abraham — so when did they have a closing of the womb? Perhaps they were at term and seized by birth pangs and could not deliver, or perhaps Abraham delayed his prayer for many days. And behold, the remedies of Abimelech and also his illnesses were not specified. And the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 20:17) — \"And they bore children\" — means \"and they were relieved\": their orifices opened up and they discharged, and this was a birth of their own. \"For every womb\" — meaning every opening. This is not correct, for even if we say regarding \"וַיֵּלֵדוּ\" that it refers to the discharge of matter, since we find the term לֵדָה in many such contexts, as in \"חָבֵל עָמָל וַיֵּלֶד שָׁקֶר\" (Psalms 7:15), \"לֶדֶת חֹק\" (Zephaniah 2:2), \"מַה יֵּלֶד יוֹם\" (Proverbs 27:1) — what the days will produce and renew — nevertheless the word רֶחֶם would never apply to any other opening. And no argument can be drawn from \"בְּגִיחוֹ מֵרֶחֶם יָצָא\" (Job 38:8), for that is a metaphor, like \"בֶּטֶן הָאֲדָמָה\" (Job 1:21). And the view of Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 20:17) is not like the words of the Rav, for he who translated \"וְאִתְרְוַחוּ\" rendered \"רֶחֶם\" according to its plain sense — \"פָּתַח וַלְדָּא\" — except that he wished to include Abimelech as well in the word \"וַיֵּלֵדוּ.\" And the language of Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 52:13): \"For HaShem had completely closed up\" — a closing of the mouth, a closing of the throat, a closing of the eye, a closing of the ear, a closing above, a closing below. This midrash of theirs derives from the pleonastic expression \"עָצֹר עָצַר\"; they did not interpret \"כָּל רֶחֶם\" as \"every orifice.\" And what seems correct in my eyes is that from the day Sarah was taken, Abimelech was stricken in the organs of intercourse and could not perform, and this is the meaning of \"לֹא נְתַתִּיךָ לִנְגֹּעַ אֵלֶיהָ,\" for נְגִיעָה and קְרִיבָה regarding women refers to intercourse, as in \"אַל תִּגְּשׁוּ אֶל אִשָּׁה\" (Exodus 19:15), \"וָאֶקְרַב אֶל הַנְּבִיאָה\" (Isaiah 8:3). And his wife and his maidservants, who were pregnant, He shut up their wombs so they could not deliver — for \"עֲצִירַת רֶחֶם\" means that she does not conceive, as in \"וַה' סָגַר רַחְמָהּ\" (1 Samuel 1:5), whereas \"עֲצִירָה בְּעַד הָרֶחֶם\" means that she does not give birth, as in \"גָּדַר בַּעֲדִי וְלֹא אֵצֵא\" (Lamentations 3:7). And Sarah remained in his house for days, and Abimelech did not turn from his evil way, for he did not understand until Elohim came to him in a dream and informed him. And Scripture did not specify Abimelech's illness but hinted at it, out of propriety and respect for Sarah. And after Abraham's prayer, Abimelech and his wife and his maidservants were healed, and the women bore children."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:32:46.358546164Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}